#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
# kbot (02 §13.1, E4-D04): only root and the Oracle Cloud Agent reach the HTTP service of
# the instance metadata, which hands out the instance principal tokens. Oracle grants their
# privileges to the whole instance, so the server itself keeps every other user away from
# it. This is a kernel rule by user, independent of cgroups and eBPF, and it covers SFTP
# sessions and processes outside systemd.
#
# The users go by number, the same on every Ubuntu: 0 is root, and 584788 is snap_daemon,
# the fixed id snapd gives to the user its snaps run as. The Oracle Cloud Agent is a snap
# and runs as snap_daemon: on the instance (17 Nov 2026) its updater was connected to
# 169.254.169.254:80, so without it the agent and its monitoring would stop.
#
# From stage E4c-2 kbot_upload, the user of the uploads to Object Storage, may reach it too:
# it has no access to the database and sees only encrypted copies, while kbot_backup, which
# reads the whole database, stays rejected (02 §13.1, point 4, E4c-2, point 1). It goes by
# name, resolved when the rule is loaded: the user must exist before (01_sistema.sh and
# 05_copias.sh create it).
#
# Only the HTTP ports: the same address serves DNS (port 53, used by systemd-resolved)
# and NTP (port 123, used by chrony) on Oracle Cloud, and those must keep working.
table inet kbot_guard
delete table inet kbot_guard
table inet kbot_guard {
	chain output {
		type filter hook output priority filter - 10; policy accept;
		ip daddr 169.254.169.254 tcp dport { 80, 443 } meta skuid != { 0, 584788, kbot_upload } counter reject
		ip6 daddr fd00:c1::a9fe:a9fe tcp dport { 80, 443 } meta skuid != { 0, 584788, kbot_upload } counter reject
	}
}
